"Aggregates dense paired observations into hexagonal cells whose color or area reveals local frequency.\n\nUse when: A large scatterplot has heavy overplotting and local concentration matters. The two axes are continuous quantities with meaningful distances.\n\nAvoid when: Individual outliers or exact observation positions must remain identifiable.\n\nVisual grammar: Hexagon position encodes a region of two-dimensional numeric space. Cell color encodes count or a declared density statistic.\n\nBuild: Choose a fixed hexagon radius in plot coordinates or a documented data resolution. Assign each paired observation to exactly one hexagonal bin. Draw cells with a labeled count scale and optionally mark important raw outliers.\n\nCheck: Check counts sum to the included observation count. Verify a shared grid and color scale when comparing panels. State whether color encodes raw count, probability, or another aggregate.\n\nPitfalls: Changing bin radius can create or erase apparent clusters. Do not compare raw counts across groups with unequal sample sizes without stating the denominator.\n\nExample: Summarize synthetic trip distance versus fare for 50,000 trips using colored hexagonal count bins.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot, 2D histogram, Density contour plot."
